<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elkab</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nekheb</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elkab, ancient Nekheb, is an archaeological site on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>. It was the cult centre of the vulture goddess Nekhbet, patron of Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Its rock tombs include that of Ahmose son of Ebana, which records the wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>, and an inscription there describes a raid by the kingdom of Kush.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elkab.jpg" alt="Elkab" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harald Gaertner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El-Kab_Necheb_1_P1120570.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
